<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-monte-carlo> {
  ex:mc-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-story-h, ex:mc-story-v ; cd:narration "The final case uses distributions to simulate a complete analytical process, not merely draw an attractive histogram. Take ten millilitres from one homogeneous collected sample, dilute to one hundred millilitres, and measure absorbance using a linear calibration. We want the concentration in that collected sample and uncertainty contributed by signal and volumes. This is not an environmental mean. The drawing is a schematic, not an instrument validation or an experimental dataset. We first calculate one nominal result, specify uncertainty models, trace one trial, then run many trials live in the existing R lab."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Live case: from dilution to concentration uncertainty"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-story-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:al-process-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:mc-model a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-model-h, ex:mc-model-f ; cd:narration "Start with the measurement equation before any random simulation. Correct absorbance for a fixed calibration intercept, divide by the fixed positive slope to get diluted concentration, then multiply by final volume over pipetted volume to undo dilution. Insert 0.200, intercept 0.010, slope 0.095 L/mg and volumes 100 and 10 mL: the nominal result is 20 mg/L. All quantities describe this hypothetical sample and linear calibration. Neither the nominal calculation nor a simulation establishes the true concentration. We deliberately hold calibration parameters fixed to isolate signal and volume contributions; their uncertainty and covariance would need inclusion in a full budget."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-model-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "First calculate the nominal result: 20 mg/L"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-model-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-model-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-model-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-equation ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:mc-assumptions a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-assumptions-h, ex:mc-assumptions-c ; cd:narration "These input distributions express information about uncertainty, not necessarily repeated physical resampling of all apparatus. Assume absorbance normal with centre 0.200 and SD 0.003. Use uniform distributions within stated volume bounds because, in this hypothetical budget, only those bounds and equal plausibility are assumed. The pipette is between 9.98 and 10.02 mL and final volume between 99.92 and 100.08. Half-width is not standard deviation; a uniform's SD is half-width divided by the square root of three. Both volumes are strictly positive. Independence is an explicit simplification, not something guaranteed by drawing three random numbers. Real shared calibration, temperature or preparation effects can create correlations. No need to force t or F into the inputs merely because we studied them."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-assumptions-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Choose input models from information, not convenience"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-assumptions-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-assumptions-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-assumptions-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-inputs ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:mc-transform a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-transform-h, ex:mc-transform-f ; cd:narration "Here the inverse CDF becomes an engine for simulation. Generate three independent uniform numbers strictly between zero and one. The normal quantile function transforms the first to a standard-normal value; scale and shift for absorbance. Uniform inverse CDFs are simple linear maps from zero-to-one to the chosen volume bounds. Fresh independent triples create each simulated trial. If all three uniforms were 0.5, the inputs would be their central values and concentration would be the nominal 20 mg/L. Reusing one uniform for every input would accidentally correlate them. The live example uses a fixed pseudo-random seed for reproducibility, approximating the independent-input mathematical model."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-transform-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Use inverse CDFs to generate process inputs"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-transform-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-transform-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-transform-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-inverse ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:mc-trial a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-trial-h, ex:mc-trial-f ; cd:narration "Trace a deliberately chosen illustrative triple, not the first seeded output. The first uniform is approximately the standard-normal CDF at one, giving absorbance about 0.203. A quarter of the pipette interval gives 9.99 mL; three quarters of the flask interval gives 100.04 mL. Put these into exactly the same calibration-and-dilution equation to obtain about 20.344 mg/L. One trial produces one possible model output. Repeat with fresh triples and store every output, rather than averaging the inputs first or repeatedly perturbing the same output value. The next scene performs that process live."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-trial-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "One trial: draw, transform, calculate, store"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-trial-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-trial-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-trial-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-one ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:mc-live a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-live-h, ex:mc-live-p, ex:mc-live-code ; cd:narration "Instructor demonstration, not an audience programming task. Run the existing browser R editor. The complete algorithm and assumptions come from the TriG CodeExample, not a renderer-owned calculation. The Park-Miller generator with fixed initial state supplies a reproducible stream; arrange successive numbers as triples, use inverse CDFs, evaluate the measurement equation and retain all concentrations. The first printed row makes one generated trial inspectable. The output mean and sample SD summarize the simulated distribution; type-seven empirical quantiles give the equal-tail 95 percent interval. Histograms approximate density; the ECDF shows cumulative fractions. Increase M to fifty thousand to examine numerical stability, then restore it and change signal SD alone to see how physical input uncertainty affects output spread. R runs locally in the browser once runtime assets are available. If unavailable, use the following deterministic saved reference; do not claim it was executed live."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-live-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Live: repeat the analytical process 10,000 times"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-live-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-live-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-live-p a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-lab ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ExerciseRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-live-code a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-code ; cd:communicativeRole cd:CodeRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasCodeExample" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:mc-result a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-result-h, ex:mc-result-v, ex:mc-result-c ; cd:narration "This is the saved reproducible reference for the default ten-thousand-trial protocol, not a field dataset or a substitute for claiming live execution. Every trial contributes exactly once. The histogram counts and summary values are generated from the authored seed, generator and input transformations and are independently replayed in tests. The simulated equal-tail interval uses quantiles of the output, not plus or minus one output SD. It describes uncertainty conditional on the input models, not an automatic frequentist confidence interval. With finite trials, mean, SD and quantiles have numerical Monte Carlo variability. More trials can stabilize them without narrowing the actual analytical uncertainty."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-result-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Simulated concentration uncertainty"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-result-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-result-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-result-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-output-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-result-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-output-info ; cd:communicativeRole cd:StatementRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:mc-scope a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-monte-carlo ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-mc ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:mc-scope-h, ex:mc-scope-c ; cd:narration "The complete chain is analytical question, input information, justified distribution models, measurement equation, simulated outputs and a scoped uncertainty statement. Normal and uniform inputs were appropriate teaching choices here; t, chi-square and F retain their roles as sampling references rather than being forced into a process budget. This demonstration deliberately omits calibration uncertainty and covariance, systematic bias, recovery and environmental sampling. A real budget must include relevant contributions and dependencies. Monte Carlo propagates the model supplied; it does not validate that model, create representative sampling, or remove bias. Its central 95 percent output interval is conditional uncertainty coverage, not automatically a frequentist confidence interval. More trials improve numerical precision, not the measurement method. Return to the relationship map to locate each distribution's role rather than selecting it only by appearance."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-scope-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Simulation propagates assumptions; it does not validate them"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-scope-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-scope-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:mc-scope-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:mc-limits ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
